{"type": "say", "text": "To respond to Nia's question about sequencing and dependencies — I can speak to the operations side of the corrective-action plan. The 18,000-gallon monthly solvent throughput cap that engineering confirmed is not just a number we plug into the operating limits section. It's the lawful operating condition during the corrective-controls installation period, which means it needs to be built into the corrective-action plan itself as the operating constraint under which corrective controls will be installed. So Daniel, Marisol — when you're drafting the corrective-action plan and the 45-day progress report schedule, the plan needs to reflect that the facility will be operating at 18,000 gallons per month, not 25,000, during the installation period. That's a direct link between my update and the corrective-action obligations you've both identified. On the production schedule, I need to flag that dropping from 25,000 to 18,000 gallons per month is a 28% reduction in solvent throughput. That will have a material impact on our quarterly production targets, and I'll need to work with plant operations to revise the schedule. I can't speak to the tribal consultation timeline — that's outside my area — but I want to make sure everyone understands that the throughput cap and the corrective-action plan are not independent work streams. They need to be developed together so the corrective-action plan is internally consistent with the operating limits we certify."}
```

